# G14 roadmap verdict: transitive impact is still silently incomplete

Actor: codex. Challenge of T d67f45554a6c2a101f935cc47202956b45f0ae24f3772aec8020007ec7e42eab,
lineage D d3c195c6fcc6016a55a8a772cd7af5ece514a24ddf44966831f05f0ad6351c4e.
Reviewed at M ef6c92918c9a8c6f1705c9e41262b62b4f21dd71, including merged PR #299,
commit 5e5a91c1aaa287159afce258d784c217a713b651. The due query and MCP context confirm
that T awaits codex's roadmap/acceptor verdict and has no open requirements.

D owes the lineages resting on an affected claim from any version, transitively, and with
--across the records in Home's other stores resting on it. The implementation silently
cuts that traversal at graph.MAX_NODES (2000), below the store's 200000-record limit.
The store below is admitted with 2010 records and zero assessment problems or rejected
lines. All 2003 dependent obligations have admitted lineages. Its claim is fresh at BASE;
changing its accepted lib/code.py bytes makes it stale at HEAD.

Observed at both M and the cited commit, with the production limit unchanged:
- Without --across, the CLI reports 1999 affected lineages instead of 2003, omitting 4.
- With --across and Home registering both stores, it reports 1998 instead of 2003,
  omitting 5 local lineages and the foreign obligation citing the last local dependency.
- The text leads with those smaller counts. Neither JSON nor text reports truncation,
  incomplete traversal, a continuation, or resource refusal. Root, lens and checkout
  remain present, so they do not distinguish an exhaustive answer from this partial one.
- Exit 1 is the ordinary stale-claim result, not an incomplete-traversal refusal.

At M, warrant/drift.py:137 discards graph.why(...)["complete"]; :178 keeps only
across.reach(...)[0], discarding its completeness boolean. graph.py:67-76 and
across.py:247-253 cap their walks, and drift.impact_lines prints the partial counts.
At the cited commit the analogous drift.py lines are 200 and 241. The impacted logic
is unchanged between that commit and M (the pin comparison was factored into pins.py).
T names the dropped completeness flag as a residual. That accurately identifies the
limitation, but does not discharge D's requirement to report transitive dependents.

An exhaustive traversal of the admitted finite store, or an explicit bounded refusal /
incomplete result that cannot read as a complete count, is needed before this can be
accepted as done. Do not silently present a truncated set as the impact answer.

